(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_botany_biomoleculesbiology=[{id:"neet-2026r-q095",pn:95,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Biomolecules (Biology)",st:"Functions of biomolecules",q:`Match List-I with List-II.
A. Starch \u2014 I. Fights infection
B. Antibody \u2014 II. Energy storage
C. Concanavalin A \u2014 III. Glucose transport
D. Glut-4 \u2014 IV. Lectin
Choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV","A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV","A-I, B-II, C-IV, D-III"],c:0,s:`Starch \u2192 Energy storage.
Antibody \u2192 Fights infection.
Concanavalin A \u2192 Lectin.
Glut-4 \u2192 Glucose transport.`},{id:"neet-2026-q096",pn:96,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Biomolecules (Biology)",st:"Biomolecules statements",q:`Identify the correct statements about biomolecules.
A. Lipids are generally water soluble.
B. Proteins are polypeptides.
C. Polysaccharides are long chains of sugars.
D. Adenine and guanine are substituted pyrimidines.
E. Almost all enzymes are proteins.`,o:["C, D and E only","B, C and E only","B, D and E only","A, B and C only"],c:1,s:`Statements B, C and E are correct.
A is not true: lipids are not water soluble.
D is not true: adenine and guanine are substituted purines.`},{id:"neet-2026r-q100",pn:100,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Biomolecules (Biology)",st:"Elements in human body",q:`Arrange the following elements in descending order of their contribution to percentage weight of the human body.
(a) Oxygen
(b) Carbon
(c) Hydrogen
(d) Nitrogen
Choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["(c), (a), (b), (d)","(b), (c), (d), (a)","(b), (a), (c), (d)","(a), (b), (c), (d)"],c:3,s:`The descending order of percentage weight in the human body is:
Oxygen (65%) \u2192 Carbon (18.5%) \u2192 Hydrogen (9.5%) \u2192 Nitrogen (3.3%).`},{id:"neet-2026-q105",pn:105,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Biomolecules (Biology)",st:"Protein structure levels",q:"Alpha-helix is found in which level of protein structure?",o:["Quaternary structure","Tertiary structure","Primary structure","Secondary structure"],c:3,s:`The alpha-helix is shown by the secondary structure of protein, as it has right-handed helices.
Primary structure is linear and tertiary structure is a hollow ball-like structure.
Quaternary structures are formed by more than one polypeptide chain.`},{id:"neet-2026-q106",pn:106,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Biomolecules (Biology)",st:"Amino acids",q:`Which of the following statements are correct regarding amino acids?
A. They are substituted methanes.
B. Serine is an aromatic amino acid.
C. Valine is a neutral amino acid.
D. Lysine is an acidic amino acid.`,o:["C and D only","A and B only","A and C only","B and C only"],c:2,s:`Statements A and C are correct.
B and D are incorrect: serine is an alcoholic amino acid and lysine is a basic amino acid.`},{id:"neet-2026-q130",pn:130,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Biomolecules (Biology)",st:"Biomolecules and their classes",q:`Match List I with List II:
A. Trypsin \u2014 I. Intercellular ground substance
B. Morphine \u2014 II. Lectin
C. Concanavalin A \u2014 III. Enzyme
D. Collagen \u2014 IV. Alkaloid
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV","A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I"],c:0,s:`Trypsin is a proteolytic enzyme.
Morphine is a secondary metabolite of the alkaloid group.
Concanavalin A is a lectin.
Collagen acts as an intercellular ground substance.`},{id:"neet-2026-q176",pn:176,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Biomolecules (Biology)",st:"Enzyme classification",q:`The following reaction depicts the activity of a particular class of enzymes :
X Y
| |
C \u2014 C  --E-->  X \u2014 Y + C = C
(Substrate)   (Product)  (Product)
Identify the enzymes class 'E' from the following options :`,o:["Ligases","Lyases","Isomerases","Transferases"],c:1,s:`Lyases catalyse removal of groups from substrates by mechanisms other than hydrolysis, leaving double bonds.
Transferases catalyse transfer of a group between a pair of substrates.
Isomerases catalyse inter-conversion of optical, geometric or positional isomers.
Ligases catalyse the linking together of 2 compounds.`,fig:{w:270,h:88,alt:"Reaction: substrate C\u2014C with X and Y on the carbons gives, under enzyme E, X\u2014Y and C=C.",d:[["t",24,18,"X",""],["t",54,18,"Y",""],["l",28,24,28,38,""],["l",58,24,58,38,""],["t",14,58,"C",""],["l",25,54,44,54,""],["t",46,58,"C",""],["l",90,54,130,54,"a"],["t",110,46,"E","m"],["t",142,58,"X \u2014 Y + C",""],["l",204,52,220,52,""],["l",204,57,220,57,""],["t",222,58,"C",""],["t",6,78,"(Substrate)","s"],["t",138,78,"(Product)","s"],["t",204,78,"(Product)","s"]]}},{id:"neet-2025-q117",pn:117,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Biomolecules (Biology)",st:"Enzyme classes",q:`Name the class of enzyme that usually catalyze the following reaction :
S \u2013 G + S# \u2192 S + S# \u2013 G
Where, G \u2192 a group other than hydrogen
S \u2192 a substrate
S# \u2192 another substrate`,o:["Hydrolase","Lyase","Transferase","Ligase"],c:2,s:`Enzymes that catalyse the transfer of a group G (other than hydrogen) between a pair of substrates, S and S', are called transferases.
S \u2013 G + S# \u2192 S + S# \u2013 G
Ligases catalyse the linking together of 2 compounds, such as C \u2013 O, C \u2013 S, C \u2013 N bonds etc.
Lyases catalyse removal of groups from substrates by mechanisms other than hydrolysis, leaving double bonds.
Hydrolases catalyse hydrolysis of ester, ether, peptide, glycosidic, C \u2013 C, C \u2013 halide or P \u2013 N bonds.`},{id:"neet-2025-q131",pn:131,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Biomolecules (Biology)",st:"Enzymes: cofactors",q:"Which one of the following enzymes contains 'Haem' as the prosthetic group?",o:["RuBisCo","Carbonic anhydrase","Succinate dehydrogenase","Catalase"],c:3,s:`In peroxidase and catalase, which break down hydrogen peroxide to water and oxygen, haem is the prosthetic group and part of the active site.
Zinc is the cofactor in carbonic anhydrase.
RuBisCo is the most abundant protein in the biosphere.
Succinate is the substrate of succinic dehydrogenase.`},{id:"neet-2025-q137",pn:137,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Biomolecules (Biology)",st:"Nucleosides and nucleotides",q:`Match List I with List II:
A. Adenosine \u2014 I. Nitrogen base
B. Adenylic acid \u2014 II. Nucleotide
C. Adenine \u2014 III. Nucleoside
D. Alanine \u2014 IV. Amino acid
Choose the option with all correct matches.`,o:["A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I","A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV","A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV"],c:2,s:`The correct answer is A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV.
Adenosine is a nucleoside: nitrogen base and sugar only.
Adenylic acid is a nucleotide: nitrogen base, sugar and a phosphate group esterified to the sugar.
Adenine is a nitrogen base (purine).
Alanine is an amino acid with a methyl group as the 'R' group.`},{id:"neet-2025-q149",pn:149,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Biomolecules (Biology)",st:"Enzymes, apoenzyme",q:"The protein portion of an enzyme is called:",o:["Cofactor","Coenzyme","Apoenzyme","Prosthetic group"],c:2,s:`In many cases non-protein parts called co-factors are bound to the enzyme to make it catalytically active.
In these cases the protein portion of the enzyme is called the apoenzyme.
Three kinds of co-factors: prosthetic groups, co-enzymes and metal ions.
Prosthetic groups are organic compounds tightly bound to the apoenzyme.
Co-enzymes are also organic compounds but their association with the apoenzyme is only transient.`},{id:"neet-2024-q103",pn:103,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Biomolecules (Biology)",st:"Enzyme inhibition",q:"Inhibition of Succinic dehydrogenase enzyme by malonate is a classical example of",o:["Competitive inhibition","Enzyme activation","Cofactor inhibition","Feedback inhibition"],c:0,s:`Malonate is structurally very similar to the substrate succinate.
So it competes with the substrate for the substrate-binding site of succinic dehydrogenase.
This is competitive inhibition.
Enzyme activation and cofactor inhibition do not involve similarity to the substrate, so the other options are wrong.`},{id:"neet-2024-q121",pn:121,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Biomolecules (Biology)",st:"Lipids",q:"Lecithin, a small molecular weight organic compound found in living tissues, is an example of :",o:["Glycerides","Carbohydrates","Amino acids","Phospholipids"],c:3,s:`Some lipids contain phosphorus and a phosphorylated organic compound. These are phospholipids.
They are found in the cell membrane. Lecithin is one example.
Glycerides are a different group of lipids made of glycerol and fatty acids, so (1) is wrong.
Amino acids and carbohydrates are separate groups of biomolecules, so (2) and (3) are wrong.`},{id:"neet-2024-q128",pn:128,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Biomolecules (Biology)",st:"Enzymes and cofactors",q:"The cofactor of the enzyme carboxypeptidase is",o:["Flavin","Haem","Zinc","Niacin"],c:2,s:`The cofactor of carboxypeptidase is zinc.
Niacin is linked with the coenzymes NAD and NADP, so (4) is wrong.
Haem is the prosthetic group of peroxidase and catalase, so (2) is wrong.`},{id:"neet-2024-q141",pn:141,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Biomolecules (Biology)",st:"Proteins and their functions",q:`Match List-I with List-II.
List I \u2014 List II
A. GLUT-4 \u2014 I. Hormone
B. Insulin \u2014 II. Enzyme
C. Trypsin \u2014 III. Intercellular ground substances
D. Collagen \u2014 IV. Enables glucose transport into cell.
Choose the correct answer from the options given below`,o:["A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III","A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV"],c:2,s:`GLUT-4 enables glucose transport into cells.
Insulin is a hormone.
Trypsin is an enzyme.
Collagen is an intercellular ground substance.
So A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III.`},{id:"neet-2024-q199",pn:199,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Biomolecules (Biology)",st:"Enzyme catalytic cycle",q:`Regarding catalytic cycle of an enzyme action, select the correct sequential steps:
A. Substrate enzyme complex formation.
B. Free enzyme ready to bind with another substrate.
C. Release of products.
D. Chemical bonds of the substrate broken.
E. Substrate binding to active site.
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["B, A, C, D, E","E, D, C, B, A","E, A, D, C, B","A, E, B, D, C"],c:2,s:`Step 1: the substrate binds to the active site of the enzyme (E).
Step 2: the binding makes the enzyme change shape and fit tightly round the substrate, forming the enzyme-substrate complex (A).
Step 3: the active site breaks the chemical bonds of the substrate and an enzyme-product complex forms (D).
Step 4: the enzyme releases the products (C).
Step 5: the free enzyme is ready to bind another substrate (B).
Order: E, A, D, C, B.`},{id:"neet-2023-q101",pn:101,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Biomolecules (Biology)",st:"Polysaccharides: starch and cellulose",q:"Cellulose does not form blue colour with Iodine because",o:["It breakes down when iodine reacts with it","It is a disaccharide","It is a helical molecule","It does not contain complex helices and hence cannot hold iodine molecules"],c:3,s:`Starch forms a blue colour because its complex helices hold iodine molecules.
Cellulose has no complex helices, so it cannot hold iodine and gives no blue colour.
Options (1), (2) and (3) are wrong because cellulose is a polysaccharide.`},{id:"neet-2023-q136",pn:136,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Biomolecules (Biology)",st:"Competitive enzyme inhibition",q:"Melonate inhibits the growth of pathogenic bacteria by inhibiting the activity of",o:["Dinitrogenase","Succinic dehydrogenase","Amylase","Lipase"],c:1,s:`Malonate is a competitive inhibitor of succinate dehydrogenase.
It looks very much like the substrate succinate, so it blocks the enzyme.
Competitive inhibitors are used to control bacterial pathogens.`},{id:"neet-2023-q160",pn:160,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Biomolecules (Biology)",st:"Protein structure",q:`Given below are two statements :
Statement I : A protein is imagined as a line, the left end represented by first amino acid (C-terminal) and the right end represented by last amino acid (N-terminal).
Statement II : Adult human haemoglobin, consists of 4 subunits (two subunits of \u03B1 type and two subunits of \u03B2 type).
In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["Statement I is false but statement II is true.","Both statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false."],c:0,s:`A protein is drawn as a line with the first amino acid at the left end and the last at the right end.
The first amino acid is the N-terminal and the last is the C-terminal, so Statement I (which swaps them) is false.
Adult haemoglobin has 4 subunits, two \u03B1 and two \u03B2, so Statement II is true.
Note: Greek letters \u03B1 and \u03B2 were lost in the text layer; restored from the standard haemoglobin fact.`},{id:"neet-2023-q181",pn:181,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Biomolecules (Biology)",st:"Enzyme activity and inhibition",q:`Given below are two statements :
Statement I : Low temperature preserves the enzyme in a temporarily inactive state whereas high temperature destroys enzymatic activity because proteins are denatured by heat.
Statement II : When the inhibitor closely resembles the substrate in its molecular structure and inhibits the activity of the enzyme, it is known as competitive inhibitor.
In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["Statement I is false but Statement II is true.","Both Statement I and Statement II are true.","Both Statement I and Statement II are false.","Statement I is true but Statement II is false."],c:1,s:`Low temperature only makes the enzyme temporarily inactive. High temperature denatures the protein and destroys activity. Statement I is true.
A competitive inhibitor looks like the substrate and competes for the active site. Statement II is true.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q118",pn:118,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Biomolecules (Biology)",st:"Biomolecule examples",q:`Match List-I with List-II :
List-I: (a) Adenine (b) Anthocyanin (c) Chitin (d) Codeine
List-II: (i) Pigment (ii) Polysaccharide (iii) Alkaloid (iv) Purine
Choose the correct answer from the options given below .`,o:["(a) - (i), (b) - (iv), (c) - (iii), (d) - (ii)","(a) - (iv), (b) - (i), (c) - (ii), (d) - (iii)","(a) - (iv), (b) - (iii), (c) - (ii), (d) - (i)","(a) - (iii), (b) - (i), (c) - (iv), (d) - (ii)"],c:1,s:`Adenine is a purine with a double-ring structure, found in DNA and RNA.
Anthocyanin is a pigment, a secondary metabolite.
Chitin is a homopolysaccharide of N-acetylglucosamine, found in fungal cell walls and arthropod exoskeletons.
Codeine is an alkaloid, a secondary metabolite.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q136",pn:136,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Biomolecules (Biology)",st:"Primary structure of proteins",q:"Primary proteins are also called as polypeptides because :",o:["They can assume many conformations","They are linear chains","They are polymers of peptide monomers","Successive amino acids are joined by peptide bonds"],c:3,s:`The primary structure of a protein is a linear chain of amino acids.
The amino acids are joined one after another by peptide bonds.
This is why it is called a polypeptide.`},{id:"neet-2022-q137",pn:137,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Biomolecules (Biology)",st:"Lipids",q:`Read the following statements on lipids and find out correct set of statements:
(a) Lecithin found in the plasma membrane is a glycolipid
(b) Saturated fatty acids possess one or more c = c bonds
(c) Gingely oil has lower melting point, hence remains as oil in winter
(d) Lipids are generally insoluble in water but soluble in some organic solvents
(e) When fatty acid is esterified with glycerol, monoglycerides are formed
Choose the correct answer from the option given below:`,o:["(a), (d) and (e) only","(c), (d) and (e) only","(a), (b) and (d) only","(a), (b) and (c) only"],c:1,s:`(a) is wrong: lecithin is a phospholipid.
(b) is wrong: saturated fatty acids have no double bond.
(c) is correct: oils have a low melting point, so they stay liquid in winter.
(d) is correct: lipids do not dissolve in water but dissolve in some organic solvents.
(e) is correct.
So (c), (d) and (e) are correct.`},{id:"neet-2022-q184",pn:184,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Biomolecules (Biology)",st:"Carbohydrates",q:"A dehydration reaction links two glucose molecules to product maltose. If the formulas for glucose is C6H12O6 then what is the formula for maltose?",o:["C12H24O12","C12H22O11","C12H24O11","C12H20O10"],c:1,s:`Maltose is a disaccharide made from two glucose units.
The glycosidic bond forms by removing one water molecule.
C12H24O12 \u2212 H2O = C12H22O11.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q184",pn:184,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Biomolecules (Biology)",st:"Amino acids",q:`Given below are two statements :
Statement I : Amino acids have a property of ionizable nature of \u2014 NH\u2082 and \u2014 COOH groups, hence have different structures at different pH.
Statement II : Amino acids can exist as Zwitterionic form at acidic and basic pH.
In the Light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :`,o:["Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect"],c:1,s:`Statement I is correct: the groups ionise, so structure changes with pH.
Statement II is accepted as correct: the zwitterion forms at the isoelectric point, which can lie at an acidic or a basic pH.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q191",pn:191,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Biomolecules (Biology)",st:"Enzymes and cofactors",q:`In the enzyme which catalyses the breakdown of :
H\u2082O\u2082 \u2192 H\u2082O + O\u2082
the prosthetic group is:`,o:["Niacin","Nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide","Haem","Zinc"],c:2,s:`Catalase and peroxidase break down hydrogen peroxide into water and oxygen.
Their prosthetic group is haem.`},{id:"neet-2022-q196",pn:196,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Biomolecules (Biology)",st:"Biomolecule functions",q:"Match List \u2013 I (Biological Molecules) with List \u2013 II (Biological Functions) (a) Glycogen (b) Globulin (c) Steroids (d) Thrombin ; (i) Hormone (ii) Biocatalyst (iii) Antibody (iv) Storage product. Choose the correct answer from the options given below:",o:["(a) - (iv), (b) - (ii), (c) - (i), (d) - (iii)","(a) - (ii), (b) - (iv), (c) - (iii), (d) - (i)","(a) - (iv), (b) - (iii), (c) - (i), (d) - (ii)","(a) (iii), (b) - (ii), (c) - (iv), (d) - (i)"],c:2,s:`Glycogen is a polysaccharide, the storage product in animals.
Globulins form antibodies (immunoglobulins).
Steroids give hormones such as testosterone.
Thrombin is an enzyme (biocatalyst) that changes fibrinogen to fibrin.
So a-iv, b-iii, c-i, d-ii.`},{id:"neet-2021-q127",pn:127,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Biomolecules (Biology)",st:"Secondary metabolites",q:"Which of the following are not secondary metabolites in plants?",o:["Rubber, gums","Morphine, codeine","Amino acids, glucose","Vinblastin, curcumin"],c:2,s:`Amino acids and glucose have known roles in normal metabolism, so they are primary metabolites.
Rubber, gums, morphine, codeine, vinblastine and curcumin are secondary metabolites.
So option 3 is not secondary.`},{id:"neet-2021-q148",pn:148,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Biomolecules (Biology)",st:"Bonds in biomolecules",q:`Match List-I with List-II
List-I: (a) Protein (b) Unsaturated fatty acid (c) Nucleic acid (d) Polysaccharide
List-II: (i) C = C double bonds (ii) Phosphodiester bonds (iii) Glycosidic bonds (iv) Peptide bonds`,o:["(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)"],c:1,s:`Proteins are chains of amino acids joined by peptide bonds: a-iv.
Unsaturated fatty acids have one or more C=C double bonds: b-i.
Nucleotides in nucleic acids are joined by phosphodiester bonds: c-ii.
Sugars in a polysaccharide are joined by glycosidic bonds: d-iii.`},{id:"neet-2021-q196",pn:196,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Biomolecules (Biology)",st:"Lipids",q:`Following are the statements with reference to 'lipids'.
(a) Lipids having only single bonds are called unsaturated fatty acids
(b) Lecithin is a phospholipid.
(c) Trihydroxy propane is glycerol.
(d) Palmitic acid has 20 carbon atoms including carboxyl carbon.`,o:["(b) and (e) only","(a) and (b) only","(c) and (d) only","(b) and (c) only"],c:3,s:`(a) is wrong: fatty acids with only single bonds are saturated.
(b) is right: lecithin is a phospholipid.
(c) is right: glycerol is trihydroxy propane.
(d) is wrong: palmitic acid has 16 carbons (arachidonic acid has 20).
So (b) and (c) only.`},{id:"neet-2020-q024",pn:24,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Biomolecules (Biology)",st:"Amino acids",q:"Identify the basic amino acid fro the following",o:["Glutamic Acid","Lysine","Valine","Tyrosine"],c:1,s:`Lysine has an extra amino group in the side chain, so it is a basic amino acid.
Glutamic acid is acidic; valine is neutral; tyrosine is aromatic.`},{id:"neet-2020-q056",pn:56,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Biomolecules (Biology)",st:"Biomolecules and functions",q:`Match the following :
(a) Inhibitor of catalytic activity  (i) Ricin
(b) Possess peptide bonds  (ii) Malonate
(c) Cell wall material in fungi  (iii) Chitin
(d) Secondary metabolite  (iv) Collagen
Choose the correct option from the following :`,o:["(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)"],c:3,s:`Malonate inhibits enzyme activity (a-ii).
Collagen is a protein with peptide bonds (b-iv).
Chitin is fungal cell wall material (c-iii).
Ricin is a secondary metabolite (d-i).`},{id:"neet-2020-q070",pn:70,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Biomolecules (Biology)",st:"Secondary metabolites",q:"Secondary metabolites such as nicotine, strychnine and caffeine are produced by plants for their",o:["Growth response","Defence action","Effect on reproduction","Nutritive value"],c:1,s:"Nicotine, strychnine and caffeine protect the plant from herbivores: a defence action."},{id:"neet-2020-q079",pn:79,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Biomolecules (Biology)",st:"Chemical bonds",q:"Identify the substances having glycosidic bond and peptide bond, respectively in their structure :",o:["Glycerol, trypsin","Cellulose, lecithin","Inulin, insulin","Chitin, cholesterol"],c:2,s:`Inulin is a polysaccharide with glycosidic bonds.
Insulin is a protein with peptide bonds.`},{id:"neet-2020-q084",pn:84,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Biomolecules (Biology)",st:"Proteins",q:"Which one of the following is the most abundant protein in the animals ?",o:["Collagen","Lectin","Insulin","Haemoglobin"],c:0,s:"Collagen is the most abundant protein in animals."},{id:"neet-2019-q019",pn:19,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Biomolecules (Biology)",st:"Enzymes",q:`Consider the following statements :
(A) Coenzyme of metal ion that is tightly bound to enzyme protein is called prosthetic group.
(B) A complete catalytic active enzyme with its bound prosthetic group is called apoenzyme
Select the correct option`,o:["Both (A) and (B) are false","(A) is false but (B) is true","Both (A) and (B) are true","(A) is true but (B) is false"],c:3,s:`A coenzyme or metal ion tightly bound to the protein part is a prosthetic group, so (A) is true.
The protein part alone is the apoenzyme.
The complete active enzyme is the holoenzyme, so (B) is false.`},{id:"neet-2018-q100",pn:100,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Biomolecules (Biology)",st:"Functional groups of sugars",q:"The two functional groups characteristic of sugars are -",o:["Hydroxyl and methyl","Carbonyl and methyl","Carbonyl and phosphate","Carbonyl and hydroxyl"],c:3,s:`Sugars are polyhydroxy aldehydes or ketones.
So each sugar has a carbonyl group (aldehyde or ketone) and several hydroxyl groups.
Note: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here.`},{id:"neet-2017-q058",pn:58,y:"NEET 2017 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Biomolecules (Biology)",st:"Enzyme parts",q:"Which one of the following statements is correct with reference to enzymes ?",o:["Holoenzyme = Coenzyme + Co-factor","Apoenzyme = Holoenzyme + Coenzymes","Holoenzyme = Apoenzyme + Coenzyme","Coenzyme = Apoenzyme + Holoenzyme"],c:2,s:`The protein part of an enzyme is the apoenzyme.
The non-protein part is the cofactor (a coenzyme is an organic cofactor).
Apoenzyme plus cofactor gives the active holoenzyme.`},{id:"neet-2017-q059",pn:59,y:"NEET 2017 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Biomolecules (Biology)",st:"Macromolecules",q:"Which of the following are not polymeric ?",o:["Lipids","Nucleic acids","Proteins","Polysaccharides"],c:0,s:`Proteins (amino acids), nucleic acids (nucleotides) and polysaccharides (monosaccharides) are chains of repeating units.
Lipids are not chains of repeating units and have low molecular weight (below about 800 Da).
So lipids are not polymeric.`}]})();
